<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Austrian Littoral</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1849">1849</a>–<a href="/years/1918">1918</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Küstenland</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Austrian Littoral was a crown land of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> on the northern Adriatic, formed in 1849 from the coastal parts of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyria">Illyria</a>. It comprised the free city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trieste">Trieste</a>, the Margraviate of Istria and the County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorizia">Gorizia</a> and Gradisca, governed from Trieste. Its mixed Italian, Slovene and Croatian population made it a contested region, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> annexed most of it after 1918.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/austrian-littoral.jpg" alt="Austrian Littoral" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Samhanin · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Austrian_Littoral_with_coat_of_arms.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Much of the northern Adriatic coast had belonged to the medieval Patriarchate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquileia">Aquileia</a>, which the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> gradually conquered until the early 15th century. In the east the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> archdukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> gained suzerainty over Pazin in Istria in 1374 and over the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trieste">Trieste</a> in 1382, bought Duino and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rijeka">Rijeka</a> in 1474, and inherited more land in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friuli">Friuli</a> when the Counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorizia">Görz</a> died out in 1500. In 1511 Emperor Maximilian I annexed Gradisca from Venice.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> did little at first to consolidate these holdings, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorizia">Görz</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trieste">Trieste</a> and Istria remained separately administered until the 18th century. Charles VI declared free shipping in the Adriatic, and in 1719 Trieste and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rijeka">Fiume</a> were made free ports. Administration was unified under an intendancy in Trieste in 1730.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-campo-formio">Treaty of Campo Formio</a> gave the monarchy Venetian lands in Istria in 1797, but the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bratislava">Pressburg</a> in 1805 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-schonbrunn">Treaty of Schönbrunn</a> in 1809 passed the region to Napoleonic rule, ending in the French Illyrian Provinces. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s defeat the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire</a> regained it, and from 1816 it formed part of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyria">Illyria</a>. In 1849 that kingdom was dissolved and the Littoral became a separate crown land with a governor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trieste">Trieste</a>, divided into Istria and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorizia">Gorizia</a> and Gradisca, with Trieste standing apart from both.</p><p>The 1861 February Patent made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorizia">Gorizia</a> and Gradisca and Istria separate administrative units, and in 1867 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trieste">Trieste</a> received separate status as well. The three crown lands each had their own administration and assembly but all answered to one imperial governor at Trieste. Trieste was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a>'s chief seaport, and the coast was a resort region known as the Austrian Riviera.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The population was mixed: Italians, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a>, Germans and Friulians were the main groups. The 1910 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, which counted the language of daily interaction, gave a population of 894,287 in an area of 7,969 square kilometres. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a> annexed most of the area under the Treaty of London and the Treaty of Rapallo, and after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> most of it went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>. Croatia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenia">Slovenia</a> now hold portions of it, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trieste">Trieste</a> remains under Italian rule, and the Slovene name Primorska survives as a region of Slovenia.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Austrian Littoral” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the revolutions of 1848, the 1849 constitution dissolved the kingdom into separate crown lands: Carniola, Carinthia and the <b>Austrian Littoral</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-illyria">Kingdom of Illyria</a></li></ul></section>
